{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n61 \n \n \nWeek 10: May 10-16 \nOn GMT 130, David Saint-Jacques replaced the burnt-out ACME Igniter Tip. He also installed the ACME \nShort Thermocouple Array, which will measure temperatures near the flame. ACME returned to \nperforming Flame Design test points on GMT 137. \n \nWeek 11: May 17-23 \nOn GMT 137 and 142, ACME Flame Design test points were performed in chamber environments of \nvarying oxygen concentrations, including 21%, 30% and 40% oxygen (the remainder was nitrogen). The \ntest points used gaseous ethylene fuel, which was sometimes diluted with nitrogen. \n \nOn GMT 140, Nick Hague replaced the bottles on CIR Manifold #2 and the CIR GC Argon Manifold to \nsupport Flame Design test points. \n \nCAPTION TO VIDEO, WHICH IS NOT INCLUDED IN THIS LOG \nThis test point video first runs at normal speed, and then the end of it runs a second time at \nquarter speed to better show what is happening with the flame. This test point was performed in \na chamber environment of air (21% oxygen/79% nitrogen). The flame almost completely \nextinguishes but then dramatically flashes back reestablishing the spherical flame before finally \nextinguishing. The asymmetry of this test\u2019s flashback makes the flame\u2019s death even more \ndramatic. The flame is brighter as it flashes back due to the added mixing of the oxygen and fuel \nthat occurs where the flame has partially quenched. \n \nWeek 13: May 31 - June 6 \nOn GMT 150, ACME Flame Design test points were performed in chamber environments ranging from 29-\n40% oxygen (the remainder was nitrogen). The test points used gaseous ethylene fuel, which was \nsometimes diluted with nitrogen. These flames were more robust and were often relatively stable in \ncomparison to the earlier tests that had been conducted in ~21% oxygen. \n \nOn GMT 151, Nick Hague replaced the ACME Bypass Controller and the CIR Manifold #2 Bottle to support \nFlame Design test points with faster nitrogen flow rates and in oxygen concentrations up to 40%. \n \n \nThis figure shows a dim blue spherical flame with glowing soot that was created at ignition.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 61}}